{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795498171425,"sessionID":"ses_df4027bf4fferxSUxHfY0Zqzpi","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-934ae7029abc154e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /travail && sed -n '30,90p' work/test_txt/dos-001__piece-01.txt"},"output":"steady flow was obtained.\nCourse of the visit\nThe equipment runs on two shifts, five days a week, for liquid product packaging. The night shift was informed of the adjustments\nmade. The service request was received on 15 November 2024. The customer's head office is in Dundee. PLC settings were not\nchanged during the visit. Dried product residue on the frame was cleaned off.\nChecks were carried out with calibrated instruments; certificates are available to the customer. No leak was found on the fittings\nafter repressurisation.\nII. FINDINGS AND READINGS\nNo abnormal noise or heating was observed during dry runs. The work station was left clean and clear at the end of the visit.\nTable 1 - Readings\nQuantity\nValue\nUnit\nObservation\nDose as found, trial 1\n2.19 mL\nDose as found, trial 2\n2.17 mL\nDose as found, trial 3\n2.12 mL\nDose as left, trial 1\n—\nDose as left, trial 2\n—\nDose as left, trial 3\n—\nDose mean as left\n—\nmean of trials\nFlow rate as left\n63 mL/min\n\n===== PAGE 2 (textlen=0, images=1) =====\n\n===== PAGE 3 (textlen=2057, images=1) =====\nZENESOL METROLOGY\nPrinted on December 13, 2024\nsheet 3/4\nReport ZM-RI-2024-4077\nKey: 1 tank; 2 supply line; 3 dosing unit; 4 pressure gauge; 5 flow direction; 6 outlet nozzle.\nV. OBSERVATIONS\nNote, dose as left: 1.96 mL.\nThe upstream conveyor Miruldra C54 (flow 124 mL/min, pressure 1.9 bar) is outside the scope of this visit; the workshop air\nnetwork runs at 8.0 bar.\nOn arrival the equipment was rated non-compliant.\nNext preventive visit: May 26, 2025.\nField team\nQuick couplings were dismantled, cleaned and refitted with a compatible lubricant. Guards were refitted and their interlocks\nchecked before restart. Sensor wiring was inspected and connectors tightened. Declan Mahon led the visit with the support of\nOwen Ainsley, at the request of Aoife Calloway. The customer was briefed on the points of attention noted during the visit. Guide\nlubrication was topped up as per the manual. The maintenance history was reviewed in the customer's logbook.\nTechnician's opinion\nProducts dosed on this line are aqueous and of low viscosity; no hazardous product was in the circuit during the visit. Tests were\nrun until readings were stable. The manufacturer's documentation was available on site and up to date. The production manager\nwas kept informed at each step of the visit. The electrical cabinet ventilation was dusted and its filter checked. Machine safety\nlabels are legible and complete.\nApproval workflow\nAccess to the machine was granted after electrical and pneumatic lock-out by the line manager. The prevention plan was reviewed\nwith the site safety department before work started. The production manager was kept informed at each step of the visit. Cycle\ncounter readings were entered in the machine logbook. The report was reviewed on 30 November 2024. Guards were refitted and\ntheir interlocks checked before restart.\n","metadata":{"output":"steady flow was obtained.\nCourse of the visit\nThe equipment runs on two shifts, five days a week, for liquid product packaging. The night shift was informed of the adjustments\nmade. The service request was received on 15 November 2024. The customer's head office is in Dundee. PLC settings were not\nchanged during the visit. Dried product residue on the frame was cleaned off.\nChecks were carried out with calibrated instruments; certificates are available to the customer. No leak was found on the fittings\nafter repressurisation.\nII. FINDINGS AND READINGS\nNo abnormal noise or heating was observed during dry runs. The work station was left clean and clear at the end of the visit.\nTable 1 - Readings\nQuantity\nValue\nUn